  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 AM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nToday - Saturday: Cooler with More Sun\n\nSkies are clearing across the region as upper level ridging \nbuilds from the west. A northerly fetch from Lake Superior will \nusher periods of clouds southward through the day in conjunction\nwith afternoon cumulus development; however, look for far more \nsun than we've seen compared to the last five days. With \ncontinued cold air advection, highs today (mid to upper 40s) \nwill actually hover a few degrees below average for this time of\nyear. Surface high pressure builds southeastward throughout the\nday and will be overhead tonight. Lows should fall to the \nlowest values of the fall season thus far with many locales \nfalling into the 20s and favored bog/sandy soil regions in \nwestern Wisconsin falling into the teens.\n\nSunday into Next Week: Warmer and Continued Dry\n\nWith heights aloft building through Monday, highs will return \nto solidly above normal, rising to around 60 for Monday (70-90% \nchance along and south of I-94 per 06.01z NBM). The arrival of a\npair of upper troughs, the first Monday night and the second \nWednesday, will moderate temperatures somewhat, with highs in \nthe 50s favored next Tuesday and Wednesday, still a bit above \nnormal.\n\nAs for precipitation, available moisture with each of the \naforementioned waves is lacking, particularly with Monday \nnight's trough. However, 05.12z GFS does suggest some mid-level \nsaturation following the frontal passage associated with the \nwave next Wednesday, which could lead to a light post-frontal \nshower or two if this feature moves through during the daytime \nhours. Post-frontal showers are a low possiblity for now (<10%),\nmainly appearing in the operational GFS and a few ECENS \nmembers. Either way, impacts are not expected.",
